// dco_matrix — STRUCTURAL standard-cell DCO macro, V2.
//
// 5 stages. Per stage one always-on backbone inverter (stage 1's backbone IS
// the enable gate, a NAND2 that parks the ring when osc_en is low). Three
// banks hang off that ring:
//
//   coarse    7 thermometer rows of einvp_4   one cell per stage, weight 4
//   mid      31 thermometer single cells of einvp_1 ONE cell on ONE stage,
//           round-robin over the 5 stages, weight 0.2 — this is the V2 fix
//   fine     95 thermometer NAND2 parked loads, round-robin over ring
//           nodes n2, n3, n4, n5 (DR-015)
//
// WHY THE MID BANK IS SINGLE CELLS AND NOT ROWS. A drive bank moves the ring
// by a fixed number of megahertz per unit; a load bank moves it by a fixed
// FRACTION per cell. Coverage is continuous only if the load bank can bridge
// one drive step. V1 put weight-2 rows against a 31-cell load bank: a 44.7 MHz
// step against a 6.7 MHz span, so the reachable set was 128 islands with
// 43 dead zones between them and 66 % of the range unreachable — the 500 MHz
// target landed in one of the gaps. A cell on ONE stage shortens one of 5
// stage delays, so its weight is 0.2 instead of 2, which is the whole fix.
// The fine bank is 95 load cells. No frequencies are quoted in this header:
// it is generated before M2 runs (M2's gate is that this file is fresh), so it
// cannot read PS-2 without a circular dependency, and the seeded numbers that
// used to sit here went stale twice over. The reachable range, the overlap
// margins and the dead-zone count all live in `results/m2/PS2.json` and
// `docs/decisions/DR-018_results.md`. See docs/decisions/DR-018.
//
// Frequency rises with drive code and rises with fine code: the core drives
// fine_en = ~fine_therm, so a HIGHER fine code leaves FEWER fine_en bits high.
// A nand2 whose B input is high follows its A input and presents the full
// switching load; B low parks its output and removes that load. (V1's source
// comments claimed the opposite polarity and were wrong — its own SPICE anchor
// measured this sense. Stated correctly here.)
//
// Pins are thermometer buses, not a binary code with a decoder inside the
// macro (DR-013): the macro is hardened SYNTH_ELABORATE_ONLY so Yosys never
// maps behavioral RTL inside it, a hand-instantiated decoder would duplicate
// logic the synthesized core already produces, and it would put a switching
// decoder inside the oscillator's own supply island. The cost is 135 pins,
// up from V1's 55, which is why DR-014's die grew.
//
